# Ordinary text normalises to LF in the repository.
*.md      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf

# ── Signed artefacts. These rules MUST come last. ────────────────────────────
#
# In .gitattributes the LAST matching pattern wins, so a general `*.md text`
# above would otherwise override what follows. (It did, on the first attempt —
# `git check-attr` reported `text: set` and the protection was not in effect.)
#
# Signed artefacts are BYTES, not text. Without `-text`, git's line-ending
# handling rewrites them on checkout, and a signed document whose bytes changed
# is a signed document that no longer verifies. The signature would still be
# present, still look right, and fail for every reader who cloned the repository
# — silent, total, and blamed on the format rather than on the checkout.
#
# `-text` means: no conversion, in either direction, ever. Verify with
# `git check-attr text eol -- <file>`; it must report `text: unset`.
examples/05-a-real-signed-document.md   -text
*.pdf                                   binary
